the permutation inequality states that for every permutation  $\sigma$  and series  $a_i, b_i$ ,  $\sum_i a_ib_{\sigma_i}$  is maximal iff we match biggest to biggest, second biggest to second biggest, and so on to matching smallest with smallest.
in our example,  $\frac 1 {a_i}$  and  $\frac 1 {a_i+1}$  match their biggest with biggest and so on, and so we get  $$ \frac 1 n \sum_\sigma \, \sum_i \frac 1{a_i}\frac 1 {a_{\sigma_i}+1}\le \frac 1 n \sum_\sigma \,\sum_i \frac 1{a_i}\frac1{a_i+1}
 =  \sum_i \frac 1{a_i(a_i+1)}  $$  which is the LHS from earlier.  $Q.E.D$ at the end, for every  $i,j$  we used a permutations in which  $\sigma_i = j$ , so for equality to hold, we must have that  $a_i$  is in the same relative place in the series as  $a_j$ , which then means equality is achieved only when  $a_i$  is constant.

